# catlog — deployment settings and secrets.
#
#   cp infra/deploy.env.example infra/deploy.env    then fill it in
#
# infra/deploy.env is GITIGNORED and never leaves your machine. `make` includes
# and exports it; Ansible reads the values with lookup('env', …). There is
# deliberately no second mechanism — no vault password file, no --extra-vars
# (which would put secrets in your shell history and in the VM's process table),
# nothing secret in any file in git, and nothing secret baked into an image.
#
# `make preflight` reports EVERY missing required key at once. Start there.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REQUIRED
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- the VM -----------------------------------------------------------------
CATLOG_SSH_HOST=203.0.113.10
CATLOG_SSH_USER=root
#CATLOG_SSH_PORT=22

# Who may reach port 22. Comma-separated CIDRs. Getting this wrong locks you
# out of the box, so the firewall role refuses to run when it is empty and
# roles/common refuses to disable password auth unless your key is already
# installed. Find your address with: curl -s https://ifconfig.me
ADMIN_SSH_CIDR=203.0.113.5/32

# --- names ------------------------------------------------------------------

# The public name. Cloudflare-PROXIED (orange cloud). This is the origin the
# game ships telemetry to, so it is baked into every issued license as the
# `htu` — changing it later invalidates credentials in the wild.
CATLOG_DOMAIN=catlog.example.com

# --- certificates -----------------------------------------------------------

ACME_EMAIL=you@example.com

# Cloudflare API token for the DNS-01 challenge. Create it at
# https://dash.cloudflare.com/profile/api-tokens with EXACTLY:
#   Permissions:  Zone → DNS   → Edit
#                 Zone → Zone  → Read
#   Zone Resources: Include → Specific zone → example.com
# A Global API Key also works and must not be used: it can do anything to every
# zone on the account, and this token lives on a public-facing VM.
CF_API_TOKEN=

# Optional. Speeds up acme.sh's zone lookup and stops it guessing when the
# account has many zones. Shown on the zone's Overview page.
#CF_ZONE_ID=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# STRONGLY RECOMMENDED
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The direct name: DNS-only (GREY cloud), pointing straight at the VM. It exists
# so that one curl distinguishes "our origin is broken" from "the edge is
# broken" — the single most useful thing to have when Cloudflare is in front and
# something is wrong.
#
# It is included in the certificate and is reachable ONLY from
# CATLOG_DIRECT_ALLOW_CIDRS, at both the firewall and in nginx. A name that
# bypasses the DDoS front door must not be open to the internet.
#
# Leave unset to have no such name at all.
CATLOG_ORIGIN_DOMAIN=origin.catlog.example.com
CATLOG_DIRECT_ALLOW_CIDRS=203.0.113.5/32

# --- the container registry -------------------------------------------------
#
# TWO TOKENS, AND THEY MUST BE DIFFERENT ONES.
#
# GHCR package visibility is independent of repository visibility and defaults
# to PRIVATE, so a public repo can (and here does) publish private images. That
# means the VM has to authenticate in order to pull — and Docker stores whatever
# credential you give it, base64-encoded and readable, in /root/.docker/config.json
# on a public-facing box.
#
# So the VM gets a token that can only READ. If it were given the push token,
# compromising the VM once would let an attacker publish a poisoned catlogd and
# own every deploy after that — turning one owned box into a supply-chain
# foothold. The write token never leaves this machine, and no playbook refers
# to it.
#
# GHCR_TOKEN — classic PAT, scope `write:packages`. Used ONLY by
#   `make images-push`, from here. Never copied to the VM.
GHCR_USER=
GHCR_TOKEN=

# GHCR_PULL_TOKEN — classic PAT, scope `read:packages` ONLY. This is the one
#   Ansible writes to the VM. Make it a separate token, not a copy of the one
#   above: `make preflight` refuses if the two are identical, because a
#   copy-pasted write token is exactly the failure this split exists to prevent.
#
#   A machine-user account is better still, if you want the VM's credential to
#   have no connection to your own identity. Set GHCR_PULL_USER to its name.
GHCR_PULL_TOKEN=
#GHCR_PULL_USER=            # defaults to GHCR_USER

#GHCR_REGISTRY=ghcr.io
#GHCR_NAMESPACE=meow-sci

# --- Docker Hardened Images -------------------------------------------------
# BUILD TIME ONLY. DHI is free but pulls require authentication, and the base
# layers end up inside the images we push — so the VM never authenticates to
# dhi.io and never sees this credential.
DHI_USER=
DHI_TOKEN=

# --- identity providers -----------------------------------------------------
# The OAuth applications' redirect URIs must be https://${CATLOG_DOMAIN}/auth/<provider>/callback
CATLOG_IDP_DISCORD_CLIENT_ID=
CATLOG_IDP_DISCORD_CLIENT_SECRET=
CATLOG_IDP_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
CATLOG_IDP_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=
CATLOG_IDP_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=
CATLOG_IDP_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OPTIONAL — sensible defaults, override when the box demands it
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# --- storage ----------------------------------------------------------------
# The NVMe volume. Everything that must survive a rebuild lives under here.
#CATLOG_DATA_ROOT=/mnt/catlog

# The block device to mount there. Prefer a by-id path — /dev/sdb is not the
# same disk after every reboot. Leave unset if the provider mounts it for you.
#CATLOG_NVME_DEVICE=/dev/disk/by-id/scsi-0DO_Volume_catlog

# Set to 1 for ONE run to create a filesystem on a genuinely blank device. The
# storage role refuses if anything is already there. This is the only
# irreversible thing in these playbooks.
#CATLOG_FORMAT_DEVICE=0

# --- the container user -----------------------------------------------------
# Must match the nonroot uid inside the hardened image, because the data
# directory on the host is owned by it. Verify once, after the first
# `make images`:
#   docker image inspect ghcr.io/meow-sci/catlogd:… --format '{{.Config.User}}'
# `make deploy` asserts the image agrees before it deploys anything.
#CATLOG_UID=65532
#CATLOG_GID=65532

# --- sizing -----------------------------------------------------------------
# GOMEMLIMIT is a soft ceiling for the Go heap: the GC works harder as it is
# approached instead of letting RSS wander into the OOM killer. CATLOG_MEM_LIMIT
# is the container's hard limit and must sit ABOVE it, so the runtime reacts
# first and a kill stays the last resort. These suit a 1 GB box.
#CATLOG_GOMEMLIMIT=700MiB
#CATLOG_GOGC=300
#CATLOG_MEM_LIMIT=900m

# --- TLS --------------------------------------------------------------------
# HSTS is a promise you cannot take back for max-age seconds. Leave it at 0
# until the certificate is proved out, then set 31536000.
#CATLOG_HSTS_MAX_AGE=0

# Let's Encrypt's staging CA: untrusted certificates, no meaningful rate limit.
# Use it while the DNS-01 plumbing is new — five failed issuances against the
# production CA earns an hour-long lockout.
#ACME_STAGING=1
